 solidified they argue that  it's not necessarily the job of a  trailer to show exactly what's going to  be in the movie but rather the tone and  feel of the movie that's an argument  that the ftc might find persuasive  additionally what little legal precedent  there is for suing movie studios over  their trailers wouldn't exactly be on  your side that woman who sued the studio  behind drive well if you've ever watched  drive you'll know that the woman isn't  wrong about what the movie is drive  isn't devoid of action but it is very  slow and yet the case got dragged out in  one form or another until 2018 when  every judge presiding over it concluded  that drive did accurately advertise the  amount of violence action and drama in  the film now that lawsuit was focused on  the movie's tone rather than any  misleading content removed from the  trailers so there may be more of a leg  to stand on here to sue morbius but  perhaps the biggest question of all is  whether the ftc would care in the first  place you see last year on our sister  location game theory we covered the  onslaught of misleading and false  advertisements in the mobile gaming  space where we saw that the ftc tends to  look especially closely at advertising  claims that affect consumers health and  their pocketbooks we basically concluded  that while yes these games were falsely  advertised the ftc has far bigger fish  to fry for morbius maybe you spent 15 or  20 bucks on the ticket that lawsuit  against match.com meanwhile some of  those unfairly advertised subscriptions  were costing people hundreds of dollars  as for that color blaster toy we  mentioned sure the toy itself wasn't  super expensive but the ftc is  especially concerned with advertisements  aimed at kids they require that these  sorts of commercials aimed at children  have clear delineation between what the  toy can really do versus the fantasy of  the advertisement meanwhile  advertisements aimed at adults or older  kids like the pg-13 audience of morbius  well the ftc doesn't seem to think that  companies need to treat their consumers  like idiots and they've thrown out  several cases based on the idea that  reasonable consumers should be able to  figure some of this stuff out like here  is this all butter loaf cake misleading  because it wasn't made entirely from  butter well no according to the ftc  people should know better especially  when all the ingredients are also listed  on the packaging instead they tend to  focus on bigger falsehoods like  volkswagen claim that their diesel cars  are environmentally friendly in that  case the ftc required volkswagen to pay  up to 14.7 billion dollars in buybacks  lease terminations and vehicle  modifications for affected customers if  the ftc were to find sony guilty and  force them to do similar refunds for  morbius movie that bombed at the box  office twice in less than two months it  would be for about one percent of what  they mandated for volkswagen and even  then it's entirely possible that the ftc  would settle with sony for an amount far  lower than the 73 million dollars the  movie made in the us and canada the case  that the ftc brought against a red bull  for example that one was settled for a  total of just 13 million dollars and  that was supposed to account for anyone  in america that bought a red bull in the  12-year period between 2002 and 2014 and  even then actually getting the money  back might not even happen just this  year in march of 2022 a judge ruled that  match.com didn't have to pay  844 million dollars of what the ftc  sought in their lawsuit against them  because of a legal loophole they  reasoned that match.com was only  publishing the misleading third-party  content instead of actually generating  it themselves now obviously none of the  cases we've talked about here line up  one-to-one with morbius even the  lawsuits about movie trailers the  specifics of how a suit against morbius  would go down how much money sony would  have to refund even if they were found  guilty in the first place are all up in  the air we just don't know because there  isn't that much legal precedent for this  sort of a case but the point here is  that this thing would be a labyrinthine  legal nightmare that's before you even  attempt to educate an elderly judge  about the multiversal implications of  spider-man no way home can you imagine  i'd like to present to the court the  following piece of evidence spider-man  killing mysterio but he doesn't really  kill mysterio it was actually caused by  illusion drones but everyone thinks he  killed mysterio which is why everyone  calls him a murderer and why those  posters in the trailer from morbius  matter so much and that's also why  doctor strange opens up the multiverse  to erase everyone's memories which is  how you get that venom joke and also why  vulture are there but it's not actually  following the official mcu rules and i  went into this movie expecting it to be  the official mcu rules clearly you see  why i was misled i rest my case and i  rest my theory my film theory and cut  [Music]  you
 

